<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Salvador</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of El Salvador</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> on the Pacific coast, bounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmecs</a>, the Maya and later the Cuzcatlecs inhabited the territory before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> conquered it in 1524. During the colonial period it belonged to the Captaincy General of Guatemala, established in 1609. After independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1821 it was briefly annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a>, then joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> in 1823, and became a fully sovereign republic when the federation dissolved in 1841. It joined Honduras and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> in a union from 1896 to 1898, and a civil war (1979–92) ended with the Chapultepec Peace Accords.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-salvador.jpg" alt="El Salvador" width="330" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_El_Salvador.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>El Salvador lies along the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, bounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> to the northwest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> to the northeast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> is its capital and largest city, and its population is about 6 million. Fluted stone projectile points found in the west point to human settlement as early as the Paleoindian period. Research into pre-Columbian El Salvador is limited, partly because high population density restricts excavation and volcanic ash has covered potential sites. The country is divided into 14 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> and 44 municipalities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first known Spanish visit was by the admiral Andrés Niño, who landed at Meanguera island in the Gulf of Fonseca on 31 May 1522. The first indigenous people to meet the Spanish were the Lenca of the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> and San Miguel were part of the Captaincy General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, and in 1821, amid unrest in Guatemala, the Spanish authorities signed the Act of Independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, which released the whole Captaincy General from Spanish rule. The export economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and balsam in the 16th century, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> in the 19th and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> from the early 20th, which at its peak produced about 90 percent of export revenue.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-humberto-romero">Carlos Humberto Romero</a> was the final president of a military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> that began in 1931, and by October 1979 the Carter administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, once Romero's biggest supporter, had decided the country needed regime change. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvadoran-civil-war">Salvadoran Civil War</a> (1979–1992) was fought between the military-led government and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti-national-liberation-front">Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front</a>, a coalition of left-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> groups. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapultepec-peace-accords">Chapultepec Peace Accords</a> required reductions in the army and the dissolution of the National Police, the National Guard and the Civilian Defence. A Commission on the Truth reported in 1993 on human rights violations by both sides, and five days later the legislature passed an amnesty law. Between 1980 and 2008 nearly one million Salvadorans migrated to the United States.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The constitution of 1983 provides for a democratic and representative government with an elected president serving five years and a unicameral Legislative Assembly of 60 deputies. In 2001 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> dollar replaced the colón, which had been in circulation since 1892. El Salvador is a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organization-of-american-states">Organization of American States</a> and the Central American Integration System. The state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, the Spanish for Holy Saviour, gave the country its name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “El Salvador” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It joined five states, Guatemala, <b>El Salvador</b>, Honduras, Nicaragua and Costa Rica, under a federal government seated in Guatemala City.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Recognition came only from Japan's allies and dependents, <b>El Salvador</b>, the Dominican Republic and Costa Rica in 1934, Italy, Spain, Germany and Hungary in 1937–1939, and from 1940 Axis satellites and Japanese dependents; the Soviet Union granted de jure recognition in the Neutrality Pact of 13 April 1941.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>El Salvador</b> is the only Central American country with no Caribbean coastline: its whole shore, about 300 kilometres long, faces the Pacific Ocean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Carrera’s armies intervened repeatedly in <b>El Salvador</b> and Honduras, defeating the liberal coalition at La Arada in 1851 and taking San Salvador in 1863, and Guatemalan contingents joined the Central American war against William Walker in 1856–1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In July 1969 Honduras fought the so-called Football War with <b>El Salvador</b>, a conflict of about four days over land and migration that killed perhaps 2,000 to 3,000 people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Mesoamerica, the region stretching from central Mexico to Honduras and <b>El Salvador</b>, was home to many peoples speaking different languages, but they shared a common civilisation: the cultivation of maize, beans and squash, cities with pyramid temples and plazas, a ritual ballgame, writing and books, interlocking calendars, and…</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
